WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:08.480
This episode is sponsored by 80,000 Hours. 80,000&nbsp;
Hours is a nonprofit that aims to help people have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:15.920
a positive impact with their career. Head to&nbsp;
80000hours.org/microcosmos to start planning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.215
a career that could potentially help solve&nbsp;
one of the world’s most pressing problems.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:46.240
What you’re watching right now is something that&nbsp;
James, our master of microscopes, had never seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:54.000
before. And given how much of the microcosmos he’s&nbsp;
watched, anything that’s new to him is thrilling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:02.000
to us. What he’d found were those two Loxodes&nbsp;
you see stuck together, waltzing around in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:09.120
very public display of conjugation. While&nbsp;
there are ciliates that frequently dally around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:16.481
in sexual reproduction, for others—including&nbsp;
these Loxodes—it’s just not a common sight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.758
So James kept his camera focused&nbsp;
on them, following their progress.&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:31.840
But in the process of watching this population of&nbsp;
Loxodes, James would find something much stranger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:38.066
going on in the microcosmos. Something&nbsp;
that would start innocently enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.881 --> 00:01:43.841
but that would end up in a place&nbsp;
far darker than he had anticipated.&nbsp;

00:01:44.311 --> 00:01:50.560
The scene in front of you now is filled with&nbsp;
loxodes, more of those freshwater ciliates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.272
we were just watching. The smaller&nbsp;
species are called Loxodes striatus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.711 --> 00:02:02.877
and the larger ones are called Loxodes magnus.
And that’s what we’re following around right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.396 --> 00:02:09.142
a Loxodes magnus navigating a field&nbsp;
of debris and other organisms.&nbsp;

00:02:11.286 --> 00:02:20.157
But why? In a screen full of these&nbsp;
organisms, why are we following this one?&nbsp;

00:02:21.001 --> 00:02:28.177
Well, this Loxodes magnus is large—so&nbsp;
large that it was able to eat a rotifer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.661 --> 00:02:33.941
those funny animals we often see getting&nbsp;
bullied by their single-celled neighbors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.886 --> 00:02:42.353
And James meant to quickly capture another one of&nbsp;
those instances before moving on. As the Loxodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.353 --> 00:02:51.449
turns, you can sometimes see the outline of the&nbsp;
rotifer taking up space in grayish oval area.&nbsp;

00:02:51.841 --> 00:02:57.761
But what James realized as he was watching—and&nbsp;
maybe you’ve caught on to this as well

00:02:58.303 --> 00:03:04.463
that&nbsp;rotifer is moving. It’s alive,&nbsp;
twisting and turning inside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.463 --> 00:03:11.023
the food vacuole it’s been stuffed into.
And when James realized that, he had to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.023 --> 00:03:18.223
recording. Because there are stories out there&nbsp;
of rotifers fighting back. Of them stretching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.223 --> 00:03:25.194
themselves and piercing away at the ciliate&nbsp;
that had the gall to eat them until finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.496 --> 00:03:32.856
they poke through the body that’s attempting to&nbsp;
digest them and escape. So yeah, James wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.856 --> 00:03:39.897
see if he could watch that happen, to see if&nbsp;
he could watch the rotifer triumph for once.&nbsp;

00:03:40.916 --> 00:03:49.636
If only it were that easy. But that’s getting&nbsp;
ahead of ourselves. Let’s just sit here for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.636 --> 00:03:57.360
bit and watch the rotifer at work. This is one&nbsp;
of those episodes where I’ll be quiet for a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:04.826
and then chime in as the rotifer’s journey takes&nbsp;
shape. And for now, the story is straightforward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.666 --> 00:04:11.746
The rotifer It tumbles and stretches, and&nbsp;
the shape of its captor shifts in response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.235 --> 00:04:17.631
expanding in width as the&nbsp;
rotifer refuses to be digested.&nbsp;

00:04:35.251 --> 00:04:40.051
If it seems like the rotifer is&nbsp;
sometimes putting up less of a fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.616 --> 00:04:47.256
you’ll have to excuse it. The conditions it&nbsp;
is facing inside the Loxodes’ food vacuole are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.256 --> 00:04:55.680
intense, a bath of acid meant to eat away at its&nbsp;
body and render all its multicellular complexity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:05.000
into a well-digested stew. The fact that it has&nbsp;
survived this long seems like a small miracle.&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:51.600
At this point, the Loxodes is passing alongside a&nbsp;
field of rotifers—the sisters of the very rotifer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:59.520
the Loxodes is trying to digest, And you can&nbsp;
see them making that same telescoping motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:05.840
as their trapped sibling, just from the&nbsp;
anchored safety of a piece of debris.&nbsp;

00:07:55.701 --> 00:08:03.713
Perhaps it seems like the rotifer is making&nbsp;
all this movement for nothing, but just wait…&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:10.720
This. This is the moment where&nbsp;
the rotifer’s escape begins in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:18.320
earnest. It managed to tear a chunk out of&nbsp;
the Loxodes, ripping out an escape route&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:21.841
that the rotifer’s head&nbsp;
can start to peek out of. 
 &nbsp;

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:42.000
Just watch as it keeps that movement going, the&nbsp;
gaping hole in the loxodes growing larger and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:49.146
larger as the rotifer continues its&nbsp;
work. The Loxodes seems unperturbed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.861 --> 00:11:58.501
continuing to swim as if its insides aren’t being&nbsp;
torn apart. But with enough time and enough poking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.501 --> 00:12:07.652
and prodding, the rotifer escapes its acid prison,&nbsp;
making its way once more into the microcosmos.&nbsp;

00:12:12.897 --> 00:12:20.097
This is a moment to celebrate, right? We’ve&nbsp;
been watching this rotifer try and try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.097 --> 00:12:26.880
and try for minutes, and here we are now,&nbsp;
with all the success to show for it as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:32.563
sets up a new home for itself.
That’s what James thought too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.286 --> 00:12:41.126
except that he quickly realized that something was&nbsp;
wrong. The rotifer wasn’t alone. It was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.126 --> 00:12:50.421
inside another rotifer, or rather what had once&nbsp;
been a rotifer—what had once been its mother.&nbsp;

00:12:54.756 --> 00:13:02.916
The acids of the Loxodes’ food vacuole had eaten&nbsp;
her body away, digesting and decomposing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.386 --> 00:13:11.072
until all that was left was an exoskeleton that&nbsp;
protected the young rotifer living within her.&nbsp;

00:13:11.835 --> 00:13:17.915
While rotifers hatch from eggs, there are&nbsp;
species that hatch from eggs that are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.915 --> 00:13:23.952
tucked away inside of their mothers.&nbsp;
This trait is called ovoviviparity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.421 --> 00:13:32.821
and it’s found in various amphibians, insects,&nbsp;
and sharks…and yes, rotifers like this one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.821 --> 00:13:38.966
using their body to shield their young.
And yes, the young rotifer survived.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.906 --> 00:13:50.066
But as James watched, a more cruel truth emerged:&nbsp;
the young rotifer was stuck. The same cuticle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.066 --> 00:13:56.898
that had protected the rotifer from the acids&nbsp;
of the loxodes would now hold it forever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.603 --> 00:14:02.723
rigid and uncaring to its former&nbsp;
life as that rotifer’s mother.&nbsp;

00:14:06.291 --> 00:14:10.617
The next day confirmed the valiant&nbsp;
young rotifer’s tragic end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.176 --> 00:14:14.397
stuck within the confines of its mother’s cuticle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.891 --> 00:14:22.917
there towards the bottom of your screen, no longer&nbsp;
pushing and shoving, but motionless in death.&nbsp;

00:14:26.395 --> 00:14:32.875
And as for the Loxodes, we don’t know&nbsp;
what happened to it. But given the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.875 --> 00:14:39.843
capacity of its family for regeneration,&nbsp;
it probably survived with little injury.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.726 --> 00:14:45.632
Perhaps it swam by its former meal,&nbsp;
unaware of their previous encounter

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:52.543
a battle waged, a battle lost, and the world&nbsp;
moving on around them both just the same.

00:14:58.581 --> 00:15:04.421
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

